--- note
## Don't trust a paper — verify it against your data
A protocol from a paper is a hypothesis about *our* data until it is measured. The **`paper-verify`** agent
encodes each falsifiable claim from Zebrahub + Ultrack **with its citation**, measures it against our cached
artifacts (Cellpose detections + GT lineage stats over all 199 movies), and returns a **MATCH / PARTIAL /
MISMATCH** verdict. Result: **4 MATCH, 2 PARTIAL, 0 MISMATCH.**
--- image
learning/label_figs/paper_verify_table.png
Every claim carries its source; the two PARTIALs are informative, not failures (explained below).

--- note
### C1 — cell number rises with developmental time  ✅ MATCH
The Zebrahub atlas is a continuous time-course. Our movies, binned by density into stages S0→S4, show mean
cells/frame **5,583 → 9,229 → 17,397 → 30,374 → 59,013** — a clean **10.6×** monotonic climb. Density *is*
developmental stage.
--- code
cells_per_frame_by_stage = {"S0": 5583, "S1": 9229, "S2": 17397, "S3": 30374, "S4": 59013}
monotone = all(seq[i] < seq[i+1] for i in range(len(seq)-1))   # True -> MATCH
--- shape
S0 5.6k → S4 59k<br><b>10.6× growth</b>

--- note
### C2 — the 10 hpf → 10 dpf window  ⚠️ PARTIAL (by design)
Zebrahub spans 10 hpf to 10 dpf. Our movies sit at **cells/frame 3,783–78,644** — the **early somitogenesis
sub-window**, not the whole atlas. This is not a failure: it tells us our (and the hidden test's) data lives at
the *early, sparser* end, which is exactly where detection is tractable.
--- shape
paper: 10 hpf → 10 dpf<br>ours: early sub-window<br>(3.8k–79k cells/frame)

--- note
### C3 — frequent divisions  ⚠️ PARTIAL (under-sampled)
The tissue divides constantly, and you can see mitoses in the raw frames. But the **sparse** platinum lineages
capture only **151 divisions total** across 199 movies (**0.066 per track**). Divisions are real but the GT
**under-annotates** them — so the metric's **+0.1 division term has only modest headroom**. A strategic fact,
straight from the data.
--- shape
151 divisions / 199 movies<br>0.066 / track<br>→ small div-term headroom

--- note
### C4–C6 — sparse, less-crowded, long lineages  ✅ MATCH ×3
**C4:** GT is ~2.6% of cells, `gap_frac = 0.00` (continuous), complete tracks — matches *"platinum"* curated
lineages. **C5:** labeled cells sit in **less-crowded** regions (local-density Cohen's *d* = **−0.58**) — matches
*"the sparse channel is consistently easier to track."* **C6:** median track span **38 frames** with few
divisions — matches *"selecting long, high-quality lineages."*
--- shape
C4 sparse+continuous ✅<br>C5 less-crowded d=−0.58 ✅<br>C6 long tracks (38 fr) ✅

